WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:03.542
Most supply chains look
like a one-way street.

2
00:00:03.542 --> 00:00:05.707
Factory, warehouse, store, you.

3
00:00:05.837 --> 00:00:07.538
But what about the trip back?

4
00:00:08.888 --> 00:00:13.634
Reverse logistics is the process of
reclaiming recyclable and reusable

5
00:00:13.634 --> 00:00:18.631
materials, returns, and reworks, from
the point of consumption or use.

6
00:00:18.761 --> 00:00:21.878
Those items go on to
repair, remanufacturing,

7
00:00:21.878 --> 00:00:24.067
redistribution, or disposal.

8
00:00:25.417 --> 00:00:28.846
So when something comes back,
it can take one of four roads.

9
00:00:28.976 --> 00:00:30.176
Repair it.

10
00:00:30.188 --> 00:00:32.678
Remanufacture it into something like new.

11
00:00:32.808 --> 00:00:35.874
Redistribute it, maybe
as an open-box deal.

12
00:00:36.004 --> 00:00:39.497
Or, as a last resort,
dispose of it responsibly.

13
00:00:40.847 --> 00:00:42.782
HP is a great example.

14
00:00:42.912 --> 00:00:47.324
Around fifty megatons of electronics
end up in landfills around the world

15
00:00:47.324 --> 00:00:48.369
every year.

16
00:00:48.369 --> 00:00:52.848
HP collects used computers and
printers in seventy-three territories,

17
00:00:52.848 --> 00:00:56.224
has recycled about three
billion pounds of hardware,

18
00:00:56.224 --> 00:00:59.201
and uses recycled plastic in new products.

19
00:01:00.551 --> 00:01:04.031
Done well, reverse logistics
benefits the environment,

20
00:01:04.031 --> 00:01:06.987
reduces waste, and lowers operating costs.

21
00:01:07.117 --> 00:01:10.203
And it can start with something
as simple as buy online,

22
00:01:10.203 --> 00:01:11.403
return in store.

23
00:01:12.685 --> 00:01:16.375
So what should Maya do with a kayak
that comes back with a scratched hull?

24
00:01:16.375 --> 00:01:17.575
Let's sort it out.

